 "prompt": "The repository at /testbed has a bug, described below. Fix it by changing the source code; do not modify or\nadd tests. When you are done, the project's existing test suite should pass.\n\n<issue>\nTSQL PARSENAME function conversion broken after recent changes\n\nI'm encountering issues with the PARSENAME function conversion in the TSQL dialect. The function seems to be generating incorrect SQL output when converting between PARSENAME and SPLIT_PART.\n\n#### Steps/Code to Reproduce\n\n```python\nimport sqlglot\n\n# This should work but produces unexpected results\nsql = \"SELECT PARSENAME('1.2.3', 1)\"\nresult = sqlglot.transpile(sql, read=\"tsql\", write=\"spark\")[0]\nprint(f\"Expected: SELECT SPLIT_PART('1.2.3', '.', 3)\")\nprint(f\"Actual: {result}\")\n\n# Also fails for other part numbers\nsql2 = \"SELECT PARSENAME('1.2.3', 2)\" \nresult2 = sqlglot.transpile(sql2, read=\"tsql\", write=\"spark\")[0]\nprint(f\"Expected: SELECT SPLIT_PART('1.2.3', '.', 2)\")\nprint(f\"Actual: {result2}\")\n```\n\nThe PARSENAME function in T-SQL returns the specified part of an object name where parts are separated by periods, with part numbering from right to left (1 is rightmost). When converting to SPLIT_PART, this should map correctly to left-to-right indexing.\n\n#### Expected Behavior\n\n- `PARSENAME('1.2.3', 1)` should convert to `SPLIT_PART('1.2.3', '.', 3)` (rightmost part)\n- `PARSENAME('1.2.3', 2)` should convert to `SPLIT_PART('1.2.3', '.', 2)` (middle part)  \n- `PARSENAME('1.2.3', 3)` should convert to `SPLIT_PART('1.2.3', '.', 1)` (leftmost part)\n\nThe conversion logic appears to be producing incorrect part indices or failing entirely.\n</issue>\n",
